″}={x}_{3}(t).$" 13194139547687 17592186576411 true] [17592186368037 1071 "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-4{y}^{″}+{t}^{2}{y}^{′}+{e}^{t}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  | $\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} | {{field-7}} |  |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} | {{field-10}} | {{field-11}} |  |  | {{field-12}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t),$ ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t),$ and ${y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).$" 13194139547687 17592186576411 false] [17592186368134 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={y}^{2}(t)-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$</span>\n</div> where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$" 13194139547687 17592186576411 true] [17592186368134 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={y}^{2}(t)-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n| ${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,u,\\,$ where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |" 13194139547687 17592186576411 false] [17592186368134 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\boldsymbol{f}(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\boldsymbol{f}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-x \\\\ {x}^{2}+2y\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\boldsymbol{f}(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-x)=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-x)=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2y \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139547687 17592186576411 true] [17592186368134 1074 "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-x \\\\ {x}^{2}+2y\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian matrix (or the total derivative) of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-x)=-1,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-x)=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2y \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2(0) \\\\ 2(0) & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, near the equilibrium, the nonlinear system is approximated by\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u,\n$$\n\nwhere the deviation vector is\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139547687 17592186576411 false] [17592186368143 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+{y}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations above. What is the corresponding linearized system at the equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\text{ }0]^{T}?$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${u}^{′}=\\,\\,$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>{{field-2}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>{{field-4}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\,\\,u,\\,$</span>\n</div> where $u=x-{x}^{∗}$" 13194139547687 17592186576411 true] [17592186368143 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &={x}^{2}(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)